Add Stripe in Test Mode (Minimal Billing)
Video 7 Script (same tone and structure as Video 1)

Today we are going to add payments to this app.

Real payments.
With Stripe.
In test mode.

No theory about billing models.
No pricing psychology.
Just wiring minimal billing.

Here is what we are going to do.
We will create a Stripe test account.
We will add environment variables.
We will create a simple checkout route.
We will deploy.
And we will test a payment.

The tools are the same as before.
GitHub.
Vercel.
Antigravity.
Supabase.
And now Stripe.

I assume you already have the app running.
Database connected.
Authentication working.
Deployment stable.

My name is Steve.
I was a professional software tester for over 12 years.
My job was to verify that systems behave correctly in production environments.
So we wire this carefully.
Minimal.
Controlled.

Let’s start.

First step.
Go to Stripe.
Create an account.
Switch to test mode.

Inside the dashboard, create a product.
Give it a name.
Set a price.
Monthly or one-time.
Keep it simple.

Now go to the API keys section.
Copy the test secret key.
Copy the publishable key.

Back to our app.

Add two new environment variables.
Stripe secret key.
Stripe publishable key.

Add them locally.
And add them in Vercel.

Next step.
Install the Stripe SDK in the project.

Now create a simple checkout API route.
When called, it creates a Stripe checkout session.
It references the product price ID.
It sets a success URL.
And a cancel URL.

Nothing fancy.
No webhooks yet.
Just minimal checkout.

On the front end, add one button.
When clicked, it calls the checkout route.
And redirects to Stripe’s hosted checkout page.

We test locally first.

Click the button.
You should be redirected to Stripe’s test checkout.
Use Stripe’s test card number.
Confirm payment.

If it succeeds, Stripe redirects you back.

Now we commit.

Clear message.
Add Stripe checkout in test mode.

Push to GitHub.

Vercel detects the change.
Builds.
Deploys.

Now we test in production.

Open the live site.
Click the checkout button.
Complete a test payment.

Redirect works.
No errors.
Session created.

This is the moment where it changes.

You now have authentication.
A database.
API routes.
Deployment.
And billing.

This is no longer a toy project.

This feels like a real SaaS.

Still manual.
Still controlled.
Still understandable.

In the next video, we’ll tighten this up and talk about what happens after payment and how to verify access properly.

See you there.